This was intentional change https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/commit/0663f87e1689f7e7d04bafbe99df5286095573ca 

Why need to have the standard name match the display name? the display name is intended to be used for display purposes and shouldn't assume will change or match the standard names. 

@cristina-dorotan could you please clarify your scenario and why you need to have the 2 names match? Also, you shouldn't take any dependency on the display name as it can change and possibly can be displayed in different languages (even we don't support that today).

CC @eerhardt who guessed correctly what could be the issue.

@cristina-dorotan although the issue is not a direct regression in TimeZoneInfo,  it is a side effect of the change https://github.com/dotnet/coreclr/pull/25373 which avoid using the `C` or `POSIX` locales. What is happening is we use CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture to get the names of the Time Zones from the ICU library. I believe you have the LANG setting in your machine set to `C.UTF-8` which is translated to en-US_posix locale in 2.x version and that locale used to work with ICU to get the nice names. In 3.1 we are mapping this POSIX locale to invariant which ICU not returning the nice names using this locale. By the way, this issue can repro on 2.1 if setting the UI culture to invariant. 

You have 2 easy options to work around this problem: 

1- either set the language to something different than `C` locale. do something like: `export LANG=en_US.UTF-8` and then run your app. Or
2- you can do this in the code before getting the names. something like the following:

```C#
            if (CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture.Name.Length == 0)
            {
                CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture = CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo("en-US");
                 TimeZoneInfo.ClearCachedData();
            }

            // get the time zone names 
```

@danmosemsft we'll try to have some nicer solution for this issue in 5.0 but the question is, do we need to service this in 3.1? we already have a simple workaround if needed.
